{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# The bugfix-size attestation gate is unsatisfiable for any locally-reviewed bug\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`bugfix_size_gate.py` (landed on main 2026-08-03, commit 1920dc9fe5) BLOCKS a Gerrit change whose\n`rebar-ticket:` bug carries >150 non-test changed lines unless that bug has an acceptable\nplan-review attestation. Its \"cross-host verification\" design constraint is explicit:\n\n    the review bot is NOT the environment that signed the attestation, so this module verifies\n    the op-cert against the PINNED `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` keyring (never the bot's\n    own env key), mirroring `verify_required_environment`'s not-pinned arm for unpinned\n    principals.\n\n`.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` on main pins exactly ONE environment:\n`9f1c8e42-7a3b-4d5e-b6c1-2f0a9d8e7c65` (`nava-opcert-prod`), whose private half \"lives ONLY in\nSSM /rebar/prod/opcert-ed25519-key\".\n\nA plan review run by a developer signs with that developer's own env id as the DSSE principal. That\nprincipal is not pinned, so `trusted_env_keyring(principal, repo_root)` returns None and the gate\ntakes its not-pinned arm:\n\n    {\"verdict\": \"mismatch\",\n     \"reason\": \"signing environment '<env>' is not pinned in trusted_environments.yaml\"}\n\nwhich is an affirmative invalid classification, so it BLOCKS (it is not one of the fail-open\ninfrastructure arms, and it is not one of the ACCEPTED `stale-code`/`stale-head` drift arms).\n\n**So a large bug fix can only clear this gate with an attestation produced by the prod op-cert\nenvironment — and there is no way to obtain one.** The deployed `opcert` service does accept\n`{\"ticket_id\", \"kind\": \"plan-review\"}` on `POST /opcert/jobs` and signs with the SSM key, but:\n\n1. **no client exists in the repo.** `grep` for `opcert/jobs` outside `opcert_service/app.py`\n   finds only the nginx template, the terraform output description, and unit tests. Callers are\n   expected to SigV4-sign to an API Gateway endpoint; nothing in `rebar` does.\n2. **the service deliberately cannot persist what it signs.** compose sets\n   `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH: \"off\"` with the comment \"a gate's SIGNATURE append lands only in the\n   discarded per-job workspace\". It returns the `envelope` in the job record, and there is no\n   rebar CLI that records a returned envelope onto a ticket as its attestation.\n3. **the guard is a production secret.** `/opcert/jobs` fails closed without the\n   `X-Opcert-Guard` header, materialized from SSM `/rebar/prod/opcert-origin-guard`.\n\n## Evidence this is live, not theoretical\n\nGerrit change 1344 (bug `129e-2d88-cce2-492c`, 554 non-test lines) is **`Verified +1`** with its\nonly blocking finding being this criterion. Its plan review PASSED and was re-signed locally;\nthe gate's complaint moved from \"unsigned; no plan-review attestation record\" (patchset 1) to\n\"mismatch; signing environment 'ade0f2ef-…' is not pinned\" (patchset 2), i.e. the signature IS\nseen and is rejected solely on provenance. No code finding blocks the change.\n\n`129e` in turn `blocks` epic `061c-ecd1-8967-4a76`, so this gate currently blocks an epic.\n\n## Why no existing bug fix hit this first\n\nThe gate landed 2026-08-03. The only recent bug fix over the floor, `e3cd-ba36-41c3-4661`\n(229 non-test lines), merged 2026-08-02 — ONE DAY BEFORE the gate existed — and carries no\nattestation at all (`review-plan --status` -> \"unsigned\"). So there is no precedent path; 129e\nappears to be the first change to actually meet this gate.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/code_review/bugfix_size_gate.py` — the not-pinned arm's severity/classification.\n- `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` — whether developer signing environments are pinnable at all.\n- a missing client for the op-cert service (submit + record the returned envelope), if the\n  intended answer is that large bug fixes must be prod-attested.\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md` / the packaged code-review guide — neither tells an author how to\n  obtain an acceptable attestation for a large fix.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] a large bug fix authored in a developer environment has a DOCUMENTED, EXECUTABLE path to an\n      attestation this gate accepts — demonstrated end to end on a real change, not by inspection\n- [ ] the not-pinned arm's behaviour is a deliberate, recorded decision: either it stays blocking\n      and the path above exists, or it is reclassified (e.g. advisory when NO developer environment\n      is pinnable), with the security reasoning written down\n- [ ] the failure message names the remedy, so an author hitting it is not left to reverse-engineer\n      trusted_environments.yaml and the compose file to discover why a PASSING plan review is\n      rejected\n- [ ] whatever path is chosen does NOT let a change self-certify from an untrusted environment —\n      the gate's original intent (no drive-by rewrite labeled as a fix) is preserved\n\n## Fix plan (operator's design decision — implemented as current policy, not a config framework)\n\nThe attestation check must NOT gate on the SOURCE of certification. It gates ONLY on the FACT that\nan attested plan review has been completed, plus that attestation's lifecycle/freshness validity.\n\n1. `src/rebar/llm/code_review/bugfix_size_gate.py` — delete the cross-host source-of-certification\n   chain: the `trusted_env_keyring` lookup + not-pinned `mismatch` arm, the `verify_opcert` call,\n   and `_plan_review_anchor` (whose only consumer was `verify_opcert`'s era anchoring). The chain\n   becomes: attestation record present -> decodable op-cert envelope -> `compute_validity`\n   lifecycle/freshness. The verdict vocabulary shrinks to the `compute_validity` literals plus the\n   gate's own `error`; unknown literals still fail OPEN (infra/advisory).\n2. `tests/unit/test_bugfix_size_gate_ad0d.py` — RED oracle: invert\n   `test_classify_unpinned_foreign_environment_flags` into\n   `test_classify_unpinned_foreign_environment_is_accepted`, and realign the vocabulary tests.\n3. `docs/plan-review-gate.md` — new section documenting the Gerrit bugfix-size attestation gate\n   and its executable remedy (AC1).\n4. `src/rebar/_guides/passing-code-review.md` — the author-facing on-ramp for the same (AC1).\n5. AC3 is satisfied by removal: the message that named no remedy is gone, and the only remaining\n   blocking finding is the teaching finding, which names `rebar review-plan <id>`.\n\nExplicitly out of scope, and deliberately so: no op-cert service client, no configurable\ntrust-policy framework. This is the current policy, \"for now\".\n\n## Testing\n\nRED-first. The proving oracle is `tests/unit/test_bugfix_size_gate_ad0d.py::test_classify_unpinned_signing_environment_is_accepted`\nplus `::test_oversized_bug_fix_reviewed_in_an_unpinned_environment_does_not_block`, both of which\nmint a genuinely signed plan-review op-cert from an unpinned principal in a fixture store with no\n`.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` at all. Both were observed FAILING on `main` with\n`verdict == 'mismatch'` / `'BLOCK'` before the fix existed, and were held out from the implementer.\n\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_bugfix_size_gate_ad0d.py -q` — 37 passed (was 35 + 2 red).\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_review_bot_retrigger_attestation_6a81.py -q` — 15 passed; the\n  `recheck-review` re-check stays fail-closed on every non-accepted bucket.\n- Mutation check: re-inserting the exact root cause (the `trusted_env_keyring(...) is None ->\n  mismatch` arm) returns both oracles to RED; restoring the fix returns them to GREEN.\n- `pytest tests/unit -q` — 4313 passed, 9 skipped.\n- `make lint` and `make typecheck` — both clean (mypy: 432 files, no issues)."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785893200681701001,"uuid":"93b8be7a-68da-4f2c-b5cf-8bdbf768150c"}